<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tahmasp I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1514">1514</a>–<a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Shah Tahmasp I, Shah Tahmasp</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tahmasp I was the second shah of Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, ruling from 1524 to 1576 after succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> as a boy. He survived years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> factional strife, fended off repeated Ottoman invasions and agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> in 1555, which left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. He moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> to Qazvin and was a great patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> painting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i.jpg" alt="Tahmasp I" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artist circa 1575 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Tahmasp_I_(1514-1576)_in_a_landscape_(painted_circa_1575).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
